otion path completely.  \n- **K: d7-c6** – Moves black king toward center and closer to queenside pawns to support promotion.  \n- **P: b4-b3** – Advances pawn, threatening to queen soon, but may be better to coordinate with king first.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nTaking the pawn on f6 with the rook (R: f5-f6) removes white’s most dangerous pawn (one move from f7) and gives our rook more activity, cutting off white king from advancing on kingside. After Rxf6, white has only the h5 pawn, which the rook or king can later target. Given our big material advantage, simplifying into a totally winning king-and-pawn endgame with rook vs king is decisive. It’s more forcing and safer than a slow king move, because it eliminates counterplay immediately.  \n\n**Answer:** R: f5-f6'}
