iately with P: f3-f2 is tempting but loses the pawn because White’s king is close enough to capture it in 2 moves unless Black’s king intervenes. Better is to activate the king toward the queenside to support the a-pawn and restrict White’s king. **K: e6-d5** moves the king closer to White’s king (b4) and toward the a-pawn, while also being central. After Kd5, Black can push a-pawn next with a7-a5, and White’s king will have to react. This creates a passed pawn on the a-file that White’s king must deal with, while Black’s king can also assist on queenside or return to defend/advance kingside pawns if needed. This move improves king activity and doesn’t prematurely advance a pawn that could become weak.\n\nAnswer: K: e6-d5'}
