aded.\n\n4. **P: a7-a6** – More restrained, keeping the option of a5 later. Less committal.\n\n5. **P: g7-g5** or **h7-h5** – Expanding on the kingside. With connected pawns, we can create a passer there, but the white king is far, so maybe we can advance both.\n\nGiven White’s king is on b4, moving our king toward the queenside seems most logical: we can either attack the white king or support the a-pawn push. If we play Kd4, we threaten Kc4, attacking the white king and possibly winning the a-pawn’s path. The white king on b4 might retreat or move toward the kingside. If it retreats, we advance the a-pawn. If it goes to the kingside, we can switch to advancing the kingside pawns.\n\nKd4 also avoids moving pawns prematurely, keeping flexibility. Moving the a-pawn now might allow White to set up a blockade with Kc5 after we play a5, but if our king is nearby, we can break it.\n\nReason: The key is to use our king actively. **K: e5-d4** brings the king closer to the action, threatens to harass the white king, and prepares to support pawn advances on either flank. It's a flexible, improving move that doesn't commit pawns yet and keeps the initiative.\n\nAnswer: K: e5-d4"}
