 would be 3 squares away; might allow White king to infiltrate.\n- **P: a5-a4** – Advances the a-pawn, creating a passed pawn immediately. Since White has no pawn to block it, this forces White’s king to deal with it.\n- **P: g5-g4** – Advances g-pawn, but White king is far; less immediate threat.\n- **P: h7-h5/h6** – Pawn move from h7 not urgent; h5 gives space.\n\nCritical factor: White king is on b3. If Black pushes **a5-a4**, White’s king must go to a3 or b4 to stop it. If White king goes to b4, Black king on b5 is currently adjacent (b5 vs b4), giving White opposition. But Black can then move king to support pawn further.\n\n**Reason:**  \nThe best practical try to win in a king and pawn endgame is to create a passed pawn as far as possible from the enemy king. The a-pawn is already a candidate passer since no White pawn opposes it. Pushing **a5-a4** forces White’s king to commit to stopping it, possibly letting Black’s king maneuver to support another pawn (like g-pawn) or eventually capture the a-pawn’s blockade.  \n\nOther moves like king moves don’t create immediate threats; pushing g-pawn or h-pawn is less effective because White’s king is closer to them if it comes across.\n\nGiven the opposition situation (kings one square apart with Black to move), moving the pawn breaks the direct confrontation and starts creating winning chances.\n\nThus: **P: a5-a4**.\n\n**Answer:** P: a5-a4"}
