ing (king is on b5, can catch up).  \n- K: b5-c5: Centralizes king further toward the kingside to support g5/h4 pawns.  \n- K: b5-a5: Moves toward a4 pawn to escort it.  \n- P: g5-g4: Advances kingside pawn, creating a passed pawn duo (g4 and h4). This could be strong because white king is far away on e2.  \n- P: h4-h3: Also advances pawn, but maybe too quick without king support?  \n\nReason: In king and pawn endgames with a decisive pawn advantage, advancing pawns that are farther from the opponent’s king is often best, provided you don’t allow them to be easily captured. Here, white’s king is on e2, which is closer to the kingside (g/h files) than to the queenside (a-file). Therefore, advancing the a-pawn might be safer because the white king would have to travel across the board to catch it, while my king can stay central and support both flanks. However, advancing the g-pawn immediately (g5-g4) may allow white king to approach and possibly blockade (e2->f3->g4), though my king can counter by moving to c5/d5/e5 etc. But with no white pawns, my king is free.  \n\nBest move: I should centralize my king first to dominate the board and then push pawns. Among king moves, K: b5-c5 is strong because it moves toward the center and kingside, preparing to support g5-g4 and also keeping an eye on the a-pawn. Alternatively, advancing a-pawn immediately is also good. But to maximize winning chances, I should not rush pawn moves without king support when the opponent king is still active. Therefore, K: b5-c5 is a flexible, improving move.  \n\nFinal choice: K: b5-c5.  \n\nAnswer: K: b5-c5"}
