entralizes further, defends the a4 pawn indirectly (by being closer), and approaches the white king which is on c2. This creates potential for zugzwang or forcing the white king to retreat, allowing the a-pawn or kingside pawns to advance freely. The move a4-a3 immediately is possible but might be premature without king support—if White's king moves to b3, the a-pawn could be blockaded. Therefore, bringing the king closer first is more flexible and powerful.\n\nAnswer: K: d5-c4"}
