After K a8-b7, we can continue chasing.\n2. **Q: d7-c8** - Also check, from c8 attacking a8. White king moves to b7. Similar to above.\n3. **Q: d7-a7** - This attacks a8 and puts the king in check. King would have to move to b8. Then we can follow up with queen checks to drive it to a corner.\n4. **Q: d7-b7** - Check from b7, attacking a8. King would have to move to a7 (only square). Then we have Qb7-a6 next move, forcing king to a8, and then we can checkmate with our king coming closer.\n5. **K: c4-d5** or other king moves - These are safe but not immediately pressing. We should use the queen to check and force the king while our king approaches.\n\nReason: The quickest way to checkmate is to use checks to drive the king to a corner while avoiding stalemate. **Q: d7-b7** is particularly strong because after K a8-a7, the white king is trapped on the a-file. We can then play Qb7-a6, forcing K a7-a8, and then bring our king closer (e.g., K c4-b5, Kb5-a5, Ka5-b6 etc.) to support the queen for checkmate. This sequence avoids accidental stalemate and efficiently corners the king.\n\nAnswer: Q: d7-b7"}
