 help it promote while also preventing White king from attacking it.  \n  - Since pawn is on g5 and kings are distant, the immediate **K: c4-d5** or **K: c4-c5** moves the king toward the center but not directly toward pawn.  \n\nWhite’s king is far away (b7 to g-file distance large), so **P: g5-g4** seems safe and gains space. After g4, the pawn is on g4 and next turn could go g3 if unopposed, but White king may start moving toward it. Black king must then rush to support.\n\n**Reason:**  \n\nThe quickest plan to create a winning chance is to push the pawn ASAP while White’s king is far. **P: g5-g4** gains a tempo and doesn’t allow White any way to attack the pawn this turn. If we move the king first, we lose a pawn-push tempo, and in such simplified positions, every move counts.  \nMoreover, moving the king now (e.g., K: c4-d5) gives White time to move his king toward the pawn. Better: push pawn immediately, then next turn evaluate king move to support it.  \n\n**Answer:** P: g5-g4'}
