awn is still on g4, not moved yet. Then white Kd3, black Kg3? But black pawn still on g4, so black king can't go to g3 (occupied by pawn). Actually, pawn on g4, king can't go to g3. So maybe better is to push pawn first.  \nActually, after Kd4-e5, white Kc4, black Kf4, white Kd3, black Kf3, white Ke2, black Kg3 (if pawn has moved to g3 earlier). Without pawn move, black king can't go to g3 because pawn is on g4. So sequence:  \nTurn 1: Kd4-e5  \nWhite: Kb5-c4  \nTurn 2: Pg4-g3  \nWhite: Kc4-d3  \nTurn 3: Ke5-f4  \nWhite: Kd3-e2  \nTurn 4: Kf4-g2 (if possible, square g2 not attacked by white king) then pawn g3-g1=Q.  \nSeems fine.  \nBut immediate pawn move: g4-g3 now, white Kb5-c4, black Kd4-e4 (or Kd4-e5). If Ke4, white Kd3, black Kf4, white Ke2, black Kg2, pawn g3-g1=Q.  \nBoth are winning.  \nHowever, moving king to e5 first is more flexible and controls more squares. But pawn move is also winning. I'll choose the pawn move because it directly advances the pawn and forces white to react, and black king is already close enough to support.  \n\nAnswer: P: g4-g3"}
