support.  \n\nGiven that the black pawn is on g3 and White’s king is on b6, we need to use our king to both support the pawn and restrict White’s king. The key principle in king and pawn endgames is to use the king actively, sometimes by moving toward the enemy king (the opposition).  \n\nK: e4-d5 seems strong because it moves toward the white king, possibly gaining opposition, while keeping options open to later support the pawn. The pawn on g3 can wait—it’s not under immediate threat, and advancing it too quickly without king support might allow White’s king to get close and blockade.\n\nReason: Moving the king to d5 centralizes it and approaches the white king, which can create a winning opposition situation. It also keeps the pawn at g3 as a protected asset. If we advance the pawn immediately with g2, the white king might head toward it, and our king is not yet in position to support. By improving the king first, we maximize our winning chances.  \n\nAnswer: K: e4-d5'}
